WEBVTT

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:05.580
 This is Delicious Revolution, a show about food.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:16.200
 I would like us to treat each other more like neighbors, and I have a feeling, I can't quite

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:23.350
 put my word on it, but there's this great feeling when I can do something for someone not for

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:32.700
 money, and farmers, and a lot of people who aren't farmers, but who make similar amounts

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.840
 of money or similar amounts of profit margin, get squeezed.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.800
 They get squeezed, they don't get to feel that feeling very often, or they have to turn

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:49.320
 down opportunities to, yes, maybe you can call it giving food away, because this is something

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.280
 that actually feels really good to me and a lot of farmers I know in this area.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:00.360
 So I do think there's seeds of relationship building that could give us a little more something

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.140
 to hang our hat on, a little besides how much money did you make this year off your farm.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:14.030
 What about an element of like, hey, were you involved with that project that completely eliminated

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.850
 that thing that we all think is terrible, which is kids not knowing where their next meal is

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:20.850
 coming from.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:29.240
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place, made by Chelsea Wills and me, Devin

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.820
 Sampson.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.780
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.310
 chefs, artists, and activists, and who have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:45.100
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talk to friends who are deeply engaged with

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.020
 many aspects of food, and who have inspired us over years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.720
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes, or your favorite podcast app, and you

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.520
 can find us at deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:11.220
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:17.330
 This is Devin Sampson here today with Joey Smith at his home at Let's Go Farm in Santa Rosa,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.790
 California.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.980
 Joey grew up here on this land.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:27.140
 He now grows a very wide variety of vegetables bound for a CSA and for the Windsor Farmers

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.110
 Market.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.110
 This is his fifth season farming here?

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.110
 Yes.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.950
 And I've had the pleasure of seeing this field transformed from pasture to super diverse vegetable

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.300
 and flowers over that time.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.060
 Joey also works and teaches at Shown Farm, which belongs to Santa Rosa Junior College, one

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.060
 of the legendarily great community colleges here in Sonoma County.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.840
 Joey teaches hands-on vegetable farming there and recently taught a course on community-supported

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.360
 agriculture.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.340
 I've been friends with Joey since we met at UC Santa Cruz about 12 years ago, and we were

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.540
 both involved in the very exciting early days of the Community Agroecology Network.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:08.140
 I've spent some time together, or we've spent time together in rural Nicaragua, and I know

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:13.360
 that Joey's time spent in the small coffee farming community of Cotabrus in Costa Rica has influenced

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.760
 the ways that Joey sees food and farming.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.670
 And over the years, we've talked a lot about our experiences in Latin America.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.990
 Joey is also, like me, an alum of Food First's internship program.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.340
 So, Joey, welcome to Delicious Revolution.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.840
 I'm happy to be here.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:29.520
 Yeah.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.640
 Hey, can we go back to rural Nicaragua before too long?

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:33.720
 Yeah, yeah, we can.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:40.670
 I want to start, it's just about to get dark, but I want to start looking out over this field

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.350
 and just describe for me what you see.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.450
 Well, yeah, I couldn't be happier to be here.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.610
 This is the place I feel the most at home and comfortable.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.460
 And I spent most of today away from it, and so it was good to come down here.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.830
 And I'm looking at some corn that we planted on July 31st, which was a little bit late.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.360
 It benefited from several heat waves.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.780
 I think we've had seven days over 100 degrees, and the corn loved that.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:22.640
 I'm looking at a hoop house we built last year that has okra and produced lots of shallots, squash, basil.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.530
 I'm looking at our wall of tomatoes that's finally starting to say goodbye.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:34.420
 And I'm not going to go over everything I see, because I think we're at about 55 different crops.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:38.220
 But this is also my favorite time of day.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.010
 So the flowers take on a different color.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:48.630
 We're doing snapdragons and ageratum and good old zinnias and black-eyed susans.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:55.640
 And I see the last few sunflowers, because it's October 7th.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.660
 So, Joey, you grew up here, right, on this land.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.180
 Did you ever imagine you would be farming here?

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:03.780
 I never did.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:04.620
 I never did.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.700
 And we used to grow vegetables over the leach field growing up.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.370
 And I wasn't—it was important to me.

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:15.010
 I liked it.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.850
 I remember only liking tomatoes that came out of that little area.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.990
 But no, this was always sheep pasture.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:32.500
 And even after I decided to grow food, it took me a year and a half to even conceive of getting a tractor in here and growing vegetables.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:38.440
 Because I always think of—thought of it, and sometimes still do, five years later, as a sheep pasture.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:50.370
 So it's—there's this nice element of—especially the other people who come here with ideas.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:55.810
 There's this really nice element of, oh, I never thought of that, even though I feel really close to this place.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:10.280
 And I've had some really interesting people come up and talk about the upper pasture and orchard ideas and things we can do that continue to kind of stretch my ideas.

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.470
 Was there a moment when you knew you were going to farm?

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:18.410
 I don't think there was one moment, Devin.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:23.820
 I think it—I know when it happened, which was sometime in 2008.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.040
 And I was working at a nursery, and I was working with plants.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:32.620
 And I realized that I loved being outdoors and working with people and plants.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:45.310
 And, of course, I was—I was in the East Bay, this great scene for—for pushing for fairer and ecologically grown food and access to food.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:53.250
 And I just realized I wanted to grow food for—I wanted to know how to do it for more than me and my family.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.000
 And one word we have for that is farming.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:03.260
 But I really, to be precise, wanted to know how to do it.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:08.430
 And so I applied to several places to learn how to farm.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:15.220
 At that time in the East Bay, you were working—I remember, you had two different jobs there.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:19.870
 One was working at Berkeley Hort, the nursery there.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.820
 And the other one was as an intern at Food First.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:23.640
 That's right.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.060
 Yeah, they overlapped a bit.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:26.680
 Yeah, yeah.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:27.620
 Yeah, with you.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:29.460
 Yeah.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.510
 And so you were thinking about advocacy there, and you were thinking about growing things.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.160
 It's a great—it's a great point.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:42.470
 You know, I never went to college thinking I wanted to farm.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:47.330
 It took me a few years to even start studying sustainable agriculture.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:54.710
 My initial interest was politics, and I got into environmental studies that way at Santa Cruz.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.690
 And I never saw myself as a food producer.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.990
 I really looked at the people doing that.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.890
 There was still this feeling of, like, you had to be born into it.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.560
 And even though I grew up here in the country, we were not farmers.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.940
 My parents are pediatricians, actually.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.860
 But I was very drawn to the advocacy.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:27.660
 I saw myself in the fight for, in a word, real food, ecologically grown food that's accessible.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:38.330
 I'm really excited about the idea that humans can be a positive force through agriculture, this way we interact with the planet on the biggest scale.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:40.910
 And so I saw myself in that realm.

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:48.700
 And then I saw friends that you and I went to school with, and friends I'd met later, having success.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:56.990
 Ryan, Toby, a bunch of people are racing through my mind.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:04.590
 And I thought, you know, it's not—even though it's not rocket science, it's infinitely complex.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:07.610
 I just had an inkling of that then, but that's good for me.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:08.570
 That's really stimulating.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.290
 I have kind of a short attention span.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:24.190
 And so the advocacy—and I felt like if I don't know how to do this, I don't know that I can speak to it as well.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:31.470
 So at first they seemed adversarial, you know, you can be an advocacy or be farming.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:46.900
 But I feel like my time at Food First and then my time dabbling at other farms set me up really well to be an intern for two years at a farm in the South Bay and really be able to thread the needle.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.000
 I feel like I've done that pretty well.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.350
 And we all got to do our strength.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:10:01.930
 And I'm not 100% farmer and I'm not 100% advocate, but I feel like I'm a good liaison between the two.

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.870
 And here we are.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:23.430
 That's actually one of the things we were thinking about with this podcast is let's talk to people who do have both a practice about food and are thinkers about food and food systems.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:36.010
 And how does—talk a little bit more about the activism that's important to you and how being a farmer shapes the activism you do and the voice that you have.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:37.100
 Sure.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.430
 I think it's essential.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:54.920
 Here we are 15 years into this century, but I want a story we tell about this century to be that we figured out how to grow food in a way with far less or none toxins.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.850
 I think it's crazy that we put poison on our food.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:06.260
 And we haven't—the only reason it doesn't seem crazy is we have this narrative that it's the only way to do it.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.020
 And so I want different narratives.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:22.140
 And so my farming on this acre and a third and growing 50-plus crops is a sort of activism or maybe the better word is, you know, an education because I bring people out.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.120
 My farm is called Let's Go Farm.

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:30.060
 I'm trying to get people excited about the possibility of growing on different scales.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:38.150
 So I don't really try to attack the big-scale guys and women, but I'm trying to show an alternative.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.720
 So for me, that feels like it.

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:47.250
 And then we have—I just got—you know, I didn't choose to get born in Sonoma County, but here I am.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:12:01.750
 And we have this beautiful, burgeoning, small and mid-sized farm movement of a fair amount of people like me not born into farming who feel that it's accessible,

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:10.960
 who feel like it's a vehicle for their ideas about community, about supporting each other.

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:22.450
 So I could probably keep rambling, but I want to—I think I'd leave it at that, which is that, you know, it's very important for me to have this be a transparent farm, invite people out.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:26.900
 Anybody listening to this, you should give me a call.

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:36.010
 And if I may say so to answer that same question, it's why I was drawn to the junior college farm,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:45.210
 which is a really unique place where we bring lots of people out to show that carrots—you know, on one level, carrots grow in the ground and apples grow on trees.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:48.550
 There's a fair amount of people that that is mind-blowing, too.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:57.960
 Or people who were raised and think that, you know, Roundup and herbicides are the only way to control weeds and we can show an alternative.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:01.470
 So it's part and parcel.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:06.340
 It's—it's—I—I—I—I feel that strongly.

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:11.940
 Oh, boy, we didn't even get into, like, the Heirloom Expo being rooted here, but that's another topic.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:18.270
 You write these amazing newsletters every time your CSA box goes out.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:26.900
 Always about what's in the CSA box, always about what you've been doing on the farm, but also always about something broader.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:33.110
 How did you start writing that, and why is that important to you?

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:40.360
 Ah, Devin, I—I used to think that if I didn't write, I would go insane.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:41.940
 I love writing.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:50.270
 Um, I farmed for a CSA six of the last seven years, and the year that I didn't was my first year on this land,

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:55.940
 and I didn't want to take people's money without knowing I could produce food, right?

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.880
 And that's what you do with a CSA in the beginning.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:00.080
 And I missed it.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:01.360
 I really missed it.

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.080
 Um, so, I don't know.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:15.140
 I—I spent—I—sometimes I think about it while I'm farming, but usually I sit down and, um, think about how to bring people—

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:16.840
 because not everyone picks up their box here.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:20.240
 Um, and I want to bring the farm to people.

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:24.340
 I think—I think there's a really exciting potential.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:29.460
 I think, you know, CSAs are getting to be, like, almost middle-aged or adolescent or something.

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:31.760
 So maybe there's a little—the novelty's worn off.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:41.770
 But I am a huge believer in that we have so far to go in bringing a little bit of what it takes to grow food to people who aren't involved with it.

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:54.470
 Um, so whether that's saying you, too, can grow this sunflower, it's really easy, or if it's just like, hey, there's a reason that these tomatoes look different than the ones in the store.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:56.010
 So I don't know.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.770
 It's more of a—it never feels like a chore.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:04.860
 So you've taught a class now about CSAs.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.780
 It's always been an important part of your model, even that you're the—you weren't doing one.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.770
 You knew that that's where you were—you were going with this.

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:19.170
 And I think probably most people listening will have a pretty good idea of what the concept of a CSA is.

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:23.650
 But I'd like to hear from you, like, what your vision of it is.

00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:30.520
 And maybe, to put it another way, like, what you hope your students take away from your class on CSAs.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:31.690
 Yeah, thanks.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:35.830
 It's good—it's good for me to keep—to keep that alive.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:36.950
 So thank you for that question.

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:52.030
 I think a big one for my students to take is I'd like them to look at the fact that CSAs are different in 2015 than they were in 2010, and that we can assume that in 2020 they'll look different than today.

00:15:52.390 --> 00:16:01.890
 And that's not a bad thing, and we can't get stodgy and rest on our laurels and say, well, here we came up with this model.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:02.710
 Let's keep it that way.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:09.100
 Here's what's exciting to me about CSAs is it tends to be a farmer-driven process.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:13.140
 And we've studied how it's become less and less.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.240
 It's become more of a consumer base, you know.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:19.770
 Now you can check a box online and say, no eggplants ever.

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:23.510
 And I—maybe I do get a little stodgy on that.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:34.970
 I believe people, if they don't like eggplants, should take their box and knock on the door of their neighbor and bring their neighbor eggplants or find out who does want eggplants, for example.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:17:01.360
 It's—I like to focus on the, dare I say, revolutionary idea of giving the producer of one of your goods, and that most elemental one being food, giving them your support through money or labor—and here it's money—before you get a product.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.320
 Um, there's faith in that.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:04.310
 There's trust.

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:08.730
 I think we need those things more in this society.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:11.700
 Uh, and not to get too abstract about it.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:12.640
 It's not abstract.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:16.450
 They earned that money, and I'm asking for it up front.

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:22.750
 And they are sharing the risk and the reward.

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:28.010
 And, um, I think that's—there's beauty in that.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:41.920
 Uh, and then just on very practical terms, I saw in Costa Rica the opposite of that, where there were 25%, like, regularly, 25% interest on loans, 20%, 25%.

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:47.270
 This—this—this co-op that I work with could never get out from the burden from day one.

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:59.910
 Um, the more I research about—you know, I learned a lot about farming in Central America, but since coming back, you look at the farmers in this country.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:03.330
 Uh, many live under the poverty line.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:05.930
 Many farmers are food producers.

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:19.000
 No matter if they're doing 1,000 acres or 3,000 acres or 500 acres, or if they're smaller like me, they're, um, they're out—they're qualifying for—for food stamps.

00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:32.150
 Um, and so I want to—I—I—I don't want to let CSA go towards that direction where, um, where we lose that—that beauty and that—that.

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:38.670
 It's an exciting alternative to a traditional go-to-the-store, pick out what you want, and pay for it.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:45.000
 So, there's kind of a different kind of commitment to each other in—in community-supported agriculture.

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:46.530
 That makes sense.

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:48.410
 And—and what does it do?

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:53.860
 Does it do something—does it influence your farming practices, does it?

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:55.520
 And how—and what does it do for your business?

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:57.210
 Yeah, well, there's two things.

00:18:57.270 --> 00:19:04.290
 One thing I want to say is, yeah, some people—people often focus on the agriculture part, and they forget about the community and the support part.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:07.130
 And those are—those are—it's a three-legged stool.

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:10.750
 Those—the community and the support are huge.

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:12.990
 And there's flexible ways to do that.

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:22.130
 I'm so excited to see proliferations of milk CSAs and meat CSAs, um, uh, community-supported writing, my friend Maisie did, you know.

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:26.170
 Um, in a word, yeah, yeah.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:31.950
 I mean, we grow—there's certain crops that we grow, like broccoli, we don't—and celery.

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:34.390
 We don't make money off them at the market.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:37.990
 I'm not growing them for the restaurant we sell to or the co-op.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:39.250
 I'm growing them for the CSA.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:47.790
 Um, and there is—that's a two-way street in that I get to grow celery and broccoli crops that I wouldn't otherwise.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:20:05.600
 Um, and then that commitment, I mean, you know, I don't want to get too sentimental about it, but these are—you know, I—there are people in my CSA who eat my food that weren't born yet when I started the CSA, and it's only four years old.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:22.740
 Um, there's people who were in my CSA the first year who are not living anymore, and I carry them with me, uh, and I—I think about them when I'm sowing seeds and weeding and, um, deciding whether to go lay down or put another half hour in.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:36.150
 Um, how has it been—there's—there's not many people I think I can ask this to, but, um, what's it like to make a living farming five years into it?

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:44.940
 Mm, well, if I was better at record-keeping and numbers, uh, I could tell you—it could be a more informed answer.

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:57.710
 Um, but I'll—yeah, be honest, I—uh, I've spent most of this podcast talking about, um, this conversation, talking about my farm here, Let's Go Farm.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:03.720
 Um, I think the phrase is labor of love.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:15.020
 I mean, we're—we broke even last year for the first time, which means we didn't the first three years, which means any money we make this year will pay off, uh, from those.

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:27.200
 Um, but I'm really glad you asked because it's—I want to see the—the—the world I want to live in, people who want to grow food can make a living doing it.

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:35.820
 So, in one way, I'm trying to showcase that, but in another, like, less abstract way, I'm very happy working another job.

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:48.200
 I mean, I get to work with amazing students and faculty and people at this other beautiful farm, and I'm—I'm very happy having that be my main income, my spending money.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:49.820
 My money for bills.

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:54.140
 Um, and then kind of gently coaxing this farm.

00:21:54.780 --> 00:22:02.950
 Um, I don't ask it to do the things that a lot of farmers have to ask their land, um, to do.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:08.030
 Um, and I mean specifically generate a high profit.

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:10.910
 Um, so we'll just see on that.

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:19.690
 You know, I don't—I don't want to sidestep that question because there actually is an interesting distinction where maybe—so, number one, I want to be clear.

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:25.910
 I want people who want to grow food and who find an affinity and a calling towards it to be able to make a living.

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:27.970
 I don't want them to be under the poverty line.

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:30.990
 So, there we can go off in one direction.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:37.370
 But on another, I also want everybody to know what it takes to grow food.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:55.100
 And so, I see in our generation and people, um, maybe who are 18 to 40 right now, this burgeoning interest in ag to be able to do their day job and know what it takes to farm.

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:56.500
 So, a CSA is part of that.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:57.820
 Farm tours are part of that.

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:03.440
 Um, there's—I want people to feel involved in their food system.

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:07.740
 I think that's probably the only way we're going to make lasting change.

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:19.240
 Um, because we can't just hope that, you know, regenerative and sustainable agriculture is—that it's a fad that will, you know, keep—it's star will keep rising.

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:22.240
 Those that we—it's too important for it to be a fad.

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:30.930
 Um, you know, there was a whole lot of articles a few years ago about, you know, college kids going off and learning to farm in the summertime.

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:39.700
 And that had this—I was happy to see it, but it had this—the media was trying to say—trying to throw it into this whole narrative of a fad.

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:44.280
 And it's—this is—there's life and death issues here.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:50.890
 Um, and there's, um, I mean that for species and I mean that for people and the people who grow our food.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:24:01.480
 Um, so I take it really seriously and I'd like to think that—and I haven't articulated it that well to myself, so I don't know that I'm doing a great job here.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:10.700
 But I do want people who work other jobs to feel connected to the food that they're eating.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:20.100
 And so maybe the livelihood element, you know, um, maybe it gets overemphasized.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:25.020
 Um, you know, this country has something like 310 million people.

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:28.330
 I think we have less than a million farmers.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:29.490
 I should check those numbers.

00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:35.820
 Um, even if a hundred million of those people are kids, you know, we're under—we're at like half a percent.

00:24:36.260 --> 00:24:40.780
 In my wildest dreams, is that ever going to go up to 1% or 2% or 3%?

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:42.960
 Yeah, my wildest dreams.

00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:45.200
 Is that going to get to 5% in this country?

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:46.100
 I don't think so.

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:51.300
 So we should—we should, um, think about those people.

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:52.860
 Maybe they are listening right now.

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:58.980
 I'm—I'm not—I wouldn't want to be so prescriptive to say, everybody go back to the land.

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:04.170
 I think they tried that in the Cultural Revolution, and it didn't work so well in China.

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:11.190
 Um, I—but I think it's very fair to ask, do you know how your food was grown?

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:18.430
 If you don't, are there things in your community, uh, that—that can reach out to you?

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:24.450
 And not just if you're an outdoorsy type or liberal or anything, but just the basics.

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:29.270
 You eat food, so do you know where—do you know something about where it came from?

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:34.900
 As you know, that—that Chelsea and I tried for one season to farm.

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:36.220
 We were mostly farming—

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:36.680
 Paper wing!

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:39.660
 Um, yeah, we were mostly farming flowers, also some vegetables.

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:44.790
 And you helped us out a lot with a lot of advice and a lot of—of growing stuff.

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:48.980
 Um, but we decided it wasn't—it wasn't what we wanted to do.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:50.520
 It was hard, hard work.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:55.940
 And I've never eaten so poorly as I did the year that I farmed, ironically, because I never had time to cook.

00:25:56.280 --> 00:26:01.320
 But, um, I also feel like I—I learned a tremendous amount about that.

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.570
 So, I'm—I'm not part of that half a percent.

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:09.630
 But we feed a lot out of the garden, and I learned a ton about farming by trying and failing.

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:12.160
 That's a great example.

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:15.350
 I—I—yes, I think about that sometimes.

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:18.050
 Because I don't—I don't know what I'm doing in 20 years.

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:18.990
 I want to be farming.

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:30.510
 Um, but more importantly, I want to use this time to show people, you know, and not to—not to keep saying that, but to show people what—what's involved.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:33.590
 And to show people the—the beauty of it.

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:38.610
 I mean, and the experimentation you can do, and the color, and the flavor.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:48.410
 Um, and I—I just think it's gonna—there is no silver bullet.

00:26:48.530 --> 00:26:51.450
 There is no one way to fix our food system.

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.330
 And I do think it's broken.

00:26:53.530 --> 00:27:02.400
 I think that that's—um, our food system is not getting nutritious food to everyone.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:11.820
 And I do want to spend a little time talking about that today, which is that I want to walk around in a world where, no matter what your politics, food is seen as a right.

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:15.650
 And so a right, to me, means you can't—it can't be taken away.

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:16.670
 It is yours.

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:18.430
 Um, it is ours.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:21.470
 And we have a lot of work to do in that direction.

00:27:21.610 --> 00:27:24.870
 And I—and so I'm so curious.

00:27:25.210 --> 00:27:27.670
 And I think Sonoma County can be a hub for this.

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:30.790
 Um, it's just people asking lots of questions.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:31.670
 How can we do it?

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:33.810
 How can we—how can we get to that point?

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:41.460
 Um, you and I were talking the other day, like, let's not wait till we're in our 60s to—to eliminate hunger in this county.

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:43.650
 We have all the ingredients.

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:45.590
 We even have the political will.

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:52.010
 So let's put those pieces together, um, and then see what we can do from there.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:28:04.330
 Um, but if people don't—you know, if people are eating really well, uh, in part of the county and lots of other people are not, that's not—that's not—that's not—that's not what we're going for.

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:19.350
 We were talking when we were scheduling this interview, actually, we were talking on the phone, and you talked to me about this idea you have about—about steps to take, a policy to—to move towards eliminating hunger in Sonoma County.

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:21.040
 Yeah, thanks.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:23.560
 Um, it's something close to my heart.

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:39.430
 Uh, I'd rather—I don't want to run a farm for 30 years and in 30 years have things look like they do today.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:51.970
 We've made great strides, but the—the—the inequality to access to good food, to—to ecologically grown food is, um, deeply disturbing to me.

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:53.150
 It keeps me up at night.

00:28:54.090 --> 00:29:01.400
 Um, and I'm tossing around an idea that I was inspired by.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:08.850
 I learned a farm down at Hidden Villa, um, which is in Los Altos, and I don't know, maybe you can put a link up to that or something.

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:12.650
 It's a great farm that also is very public, and people should go see.

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:24.580
 And we got a grant from a foundation to grow food for Mountain View Community Services Agency, which—let's just call the Food Bank.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:38.130
 And that bit of money—and I never actually knew exactly how much it was, but it was—it was, um, it was enough.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:49.910
 It was not a lot, but it was enough to make it worth our while to grow food specifically for the Food Bank, to plan into our—our farm season and to plan into our day to harvest for the Food Bank.

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:59.540
 Um, a volunteer with the Food Bank would swing by the next day, pick it up in our reusable lugs.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:05.810
 He'd drop off the empties, bring the full ones over, and bring them to the Food Bank.

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:13.790
 And once a year, uh, the clients of this Food Bank, the people who ate food from this Food Bank, would come out to the farm.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:20.010
 And again, that's—that touched me because the story of our food is important.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:24.690
 I think it gets fetishized sometimes, but it's really important to see it.

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:33.900
 Um, so anyway, that was six years ago, seven years ago, and it's always been in my mind.

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:39.370
 And I think maybe it's a foundation grant, but maybe there's other funding sources.

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:45.650
 Um, and I'm going to leave that broad in this—during this conversation, but I think we should all think about it.

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:51.610
 Um, but the idea would be to get—to incentivize farmers to grow food for the Food Bank.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:31:01.560
 Right now, if you—if I took a survey of 10 farmers that came to mind, I'd probably get a few who bring their food to, um, food banks.

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:09.870
 I'd get a fair amount who said they wish they could do more, but they don't have the—you know, they're—they're basically losing money to do it.

00:31:10.540 --> 00:31:17.110
 Um, or there's a fair amount who say, ah, you know, I had a negative experience with people who gleaned, and they messed up my crops.

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:26.860
 And I—it's too important to me, uh, financially, and also just in a, you know, this is my farm to have people come out and—and mess up your—your plants.

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:32.510
 Um, so how do we incentivize farms?

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:35.700
 And my idea is not too much money.

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:38.170
 Maybe it's $5,000, maybe it's $10,000.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:48.720
 Um, to have farmers, what you ask them—we take a survey or something—what would it take to grow a little, you know, to grow some percentage for the Food Bank?

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:59.440
 And I guarantee you 10 out of 10 of those farmers would say someone coming along and picking it up, doing a route, would help so much.

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:13.180
 You know, once a year in this county, the—the poor male—male people pick up food for the Food Bank, and I always think of them like, that's kind of them, but—but do they—you know, that's a lot of lifting.

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:28.610
 Um, so my idea, and I'm floating it, and I want feedback on it, and I want to collaborate, and I really want to keep this down to earth and not too abstract, is like, I want your listeners to contact me if they have great ideas.

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:32.790
 But the idea is, they get a little extra money, whatever it is.

00:32:32.810 --> 00:32:34.570
 It's not $100,000.

00:32:34.910 --> 00:32:39.520
 My idea really means just a little, just to make it worth it.

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:45.940
 Because most farmers want to share their food, they want it to be appreciated, but they can't give it away for free.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:33:01.830
 And then the flip side of that is the farm or ranch or garden has to make themselves available to public access.

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:04.870
 So is that two school field trips a year?

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:13.410
 Is that bringing people out from the Food Bank to, if not glean, then to walk around and see where the food they've been enjoying all year came from?

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:24.120
 I think this—and again, it's not really my idea, because I saw it down in Santa Clara County, but we need that up here.

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:34.190
 I also have some idea—I think all food banks should have gardens, because here I'm on an acre and a third, and we're producing thousands of pounds of food.

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:40.670
 And specifically, food that you don't see a lot of in food banks.

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:42.370
 You don't see a whole lot of eggplant.

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:46.290
 You do see a lot of rice-a-roni in packaged food.

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:51.580
 So it's got to—it's not just food, you know, capital F, food.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:55.440
 It's healthy food, and it's food that people want to eat.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:34:06.000
 So part of what I love about the idea is that it's—for the poor often get the leftovers and the dregs of the food system.

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:10.960
 But this is food especially produced for people who can't afford it.

00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:12.900
 It's firsts, it's not seconds.

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:22.500
 And another thing I like about it is that it's a public investment in farms being around and serving a public good.

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:30.450
 And where it's like, I think there's a lot of people—and we were talking a little bit about that feeling of, is there a fad going on with farming?

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:54.890
 I think that one of the ways that it might be is that a lot of people with big dedications towards a more just food system end up spending their working lives growing food for the relatively wealthy people in the communities who can pay higher prices for food.

00:34:55.900 --> 00:35:05.660
 And so I really love that idea for those reasons, like, is there a public investment in both having farms around and in there not being hunger?

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:22.880
 Yeah, I really appreciate that because I—I mean, you know, I am trying to keep it broad, but I think—let's throw out on the table—one of those would be a tiny tax that basically is saying—and I would stump for this tax.

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:34.210
 And I think we could get people who are generally anti-tax on board to say, are farms valuable, you know, do we want them in our community?

00:35:34.750 --> 00:35:47.270
 And here's another one, does having—does that vision of looking around you, walking down the street, driving, and having no one lacking food, does that appeal to you?

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:52.660
 And it doesn't have to be a tax, but a tax is kind of a neat way to basically say we're all in this.

00:35:52.660 --> 00:35:57.670
 And I'm talking a hundredth of a percent—it doesn't have to be much.

00:35:58.330 --> 00:36:03.750
 And again, because I'm not saying that the farm should grow all for the food bank.

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:14.930
 I'm saying, wouldn't it be interesting if we can get farms to be more transparent, open themselves up to school kids, which they might want to do anyway, but again, not have the—

00:36:14.930 --> 00:36:21.850
 Profit margins are so thin in agriculture, whether you're small, whether you're a micro-farm, small farm, medium farm, or huge farm.

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:31.590
 There is an interesting breakdown of—it's about 99% people making very little money and 1% making a lot of money off ag.

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:40.830
 So a tax is one way that, just bouncing off what you said, can we come together on this?

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:49.960
 Can we use—can we create hubs where that food goes that maybe isn't a food bank?

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:55.690
 I mean, I always like to think about, like, not having food banks.

00:36:55.990 --> 00:37:10.600
 You know, I fear that they—the fact that if they get too institutionalized, then we are existing off this idea that food is not a right, but it's this—how would I put it?

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:15.120
 You know, if we normalize food banks, then that kind of normalizes hunger.

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:17.050
 And charity is the answer.

00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:18.270
 And charity being the answer.

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:26.510
 That you're not entitled to eating, but you're at the subject of whatever charity someone offers.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:34.070
 The kindness of strangers is a nice thing and something I think is a beautiful thing, but it's—that's not what I mean when I say a right.

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:38.770
 A right is like your right to free speech and all those other rights we have.

00:37:38.950 --> 00:37:40.610
 They're supposed to be inalienable.

00:37:42.980 --> 00:37:44.560
 It's such an interesting idea, Joey.

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:52.530
 And one thing it makes me think of is Brazil's Cero Ombre, the Zero Hunger Program, that's been replicated again in Nicaragua and a few other places.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:38:08.650
 I think it's a significant amount of the country's budget goes to eliminating hunger and the significant amount of the food that's bought has earmarked to buy food from small local farms.

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:11.000
 I want to look more into that.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:20.690
 I want to get together, again, to be very clear, I want to get together with people listening to this and sit down at a table and pool those resources.

00:38:20.690 --> 00:38:27.550
 Because I don't know much about Cero Hambre, but it sounds great and it sounds important.

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:32.770
 And can we talk to people doing that and say, oh, what's not working there?

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:37.460
 But again, with this central core idea of everybody gets to eat.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:49.110
 You know, the reason I'm focusing on Sonoma County is I live here and it's just so blatant that we have all the resources to make it happen.

00:38:49.250 --> 00:38:50.530
 It's so clear.

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:58.300
 But then the idea would be use that as a case study and look what worked and what didn't and do it in other places.

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:16.670
 So for people who aren't here, I guess this is another part of me that just because I've spent a lot of my academic energy on arguing that hunger is not caused by not having enough food.

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:31.210
 But it's caused by inequality, it's caused by poverty, and it's caused by racialized and ingrained class inequalities that make it hard for people to access food.

00:39:31.970 --> 00:39:34.720
 What does a plan, so what does a plan like that do?

00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:39.870
 I have the feeling that you're asking for something more than producing more food.

00:39:39.990 --> 00:39:41.070
 Yeah, you're right, you're right.

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:48.150
 I think it has something to do with, let's just say it's, let's say 40 farms are involved in this.

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:51.030
 You know, it's not everybody, it's not obligatory in any way.

00:39:51.490 --> 00:40:00.490
 You sign up, you say, I'm interested, and there's a pool of money, and I don't know where that's coming from.

00:40:00.490 --> 00:40:03.570
 Maybe that tax, maybe a foundation, I don't know.

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:11.530
 And it's generally folded into your farm budget.

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:16.640
 And no, I'm, yeah, I'm not, I don't think I'm saying go out and buy more land.

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:20.100
 You know, there's already, there's a lot of interesting programs that do that.

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:28.800
 I think I'm saying, I think I am saying in what those 40 farms produce, in what those 80 farms, what those X number of farms produce,

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:36.410
 we can take a significant chunk out of the deficit of food, of good food, right?

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:43.200
 I don't know if that's 100%, maybe, maybe it still involves things shipped up from elsewhere.

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:50.320
 But isn't there something exciting about sort of the kind of, isn't there something we can be proud of?

00:40:50.500 --> 00:40:53.030
 Sonoma County can feed itself, right?

00:40:55.050 --> 00:41:03.670
 But sure, it involves, yeah, I would say, to keep my answer short, no, it's not necessarily about producing more.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:05.570
 I mean, here we are in my field.

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:07.370
 I don't have room to expand.

00:41:07.770 --> 00:41:09.340
 I want more of this food.

00:41:09.510 --> 00:41:17.240
 I want the food that I produce, which is part of the labor that I put into it, to go to fix this problem.

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:17.880
 It's a problem.

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:21.060
 I want people to be talking about the fact that it's a problem.

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:29.580
 I don't think I'm saying, you know, annex the neighbor's land and do more there.

00:41:29.680 --> 00:41:32.010
 And I'm curious about that.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:37.670
 I want to sit down at that table and look at maps of farms and look at, really talk to farmers.

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:42.070
 How much food, what do you think, went to waste on your farm last year?

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:46.860
 And start bouncing numbers around.

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:48.730
 Yeah.

00:41:49.740 --> 00:42:00.960
 It seems to me like you talked about CSAs being, I think you said there's a revolutionary way that it changes the relationship between the food buyer and the food producer.

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:15.320
 Is it possible that there's something equally revolutionary possible about the relationship between a food grower and people who are now recipients of charity?

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:20.610
 That is a great question.

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:35.620
 You know, I want us to, I would like us to treat each other more like neighbors.

00:42:36.140 --> 00:42:49.910
 And I have a feeling, I can't quite put my word on it, but there's this great feeling when I can do something for someone not for money.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:43:02.560
 And farmers and a lot of people who aren't farmers, but who make similar amounts of money or similar amounts of profit margins, get squeezed.

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:06.160
 And they get squeezed, they don't get to feel that feeling very often.

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:13.450
 Or they have to turn down opportunities to, yes, maybe you can call it giving food away.

00:43:13.450 --> 00:43:19.700
 Because this is something that actually feels really good to me and a lot of farmers I know in this area.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:25.740
 But it's impractical, every business class is telling you not to do that.

00:43:26.730 --> 00:43:42.500
 So I do think there's seeds of relationship building that could give us a little more something to hang our hat on, a little besides how much money did you make this year off your farm.

00:43:43.280 --> 00:44:02.680
 What about an element of like, hey, were you in, you know, were you in, were you involved with that project that, that completely eliminated that thing that we all think is terrible, which is kids not knowing where their next meal is coming from.

00:44:04.610 --> 00:44:09.960
 Seniors, not knowing where their next meal is coming from.

00:44:11.270 --> 00:44:14.370
 People between, you know, the rest of people.

00:44:14.870 --> 00:44:22.020
 The last I heard is one in six people in this county, this wealthy county are on food assistance.

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:25.430
 And maybe it's one in seven.

00:44:25.570 --> 00:44:25.870
 One in seven.

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:27.250
 I think I saw that on a billboard.

00:44:29.710 --> 00:44:39.580
 You know, so I do think there's seeds in that CSA model, which is that here these people gave me money and I'm giving them food.

00:44:40.220 --> 00:44:48.680
 And one, one way of saying that is they bought food for me, but I love that idea that maybe we don't have to describe it that way.

00:44:49.220 --> 00:44:50.220
 I fed you.

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:53.310
 You allowed me to do the work I love.

00:44:53.310 --> 00:45:13.830
 I want to talk about a couple of people who have been influential and as farmers to you, both here, right, just a walk from this farm and also in far away in Costa Rica, especially because you have this vision of a more just world.

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:22.230
 And you have this conviction that being a farmer is a good place to come at that from.

00:45:22.810 --> 00:45:26.990
 And what did you learn from your neighbor, Dick Wagner?

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:29.110
 What did you learn from people in Costa Rica about that?

00:45:31.210 --> 00:45:32.590
 Thank you for evoking him.

00:45:33.110 --> 00:45:37.610
 Oh, Dick, we're going to go visit him Monday.

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:41.510
 He used to live right down the road.

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:46.530
 He taught my parents everything they knew about raising sheep.

00:45:48.700 --> 00:45:54.400
 Dick raised, Dick and his wife raised goats, sheep and pigs.

00:45:55.100 --> 00:45:56.550
 It's very interesting, actually.

00:45:58.540 --> 00:46:01.860
 This man, he had a former life of as an auto mechanic.

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:03.420
 I don't know if I ever told you that.

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:06.220
 Down in Southern California, Redondo Beach.

00:46:08.140 --> 00:46:14.330
 And like many people in the early 70s, he wanted to work the land and he didn't know much about it.

00:46:15.530 --> 00:46:21.210
 Anyway, I can go, I can already see myself going off, going on for a half hour about Dick.

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:25.230
 But I, Dick and Barbara, because they were very much a team.

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:31.150
 Well, let me ask you, what was their reaction when you started farming here?

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:43.320
 Oh, well, I remember Dick saying, he often said, no one wants to farm and we're the last farm in this valley.

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:49.800
 And even after a couple of years of me farming this valley, he kept saying that.

00:46:49.860 --> 00:46:58.420
 And I kept trying to point out that, you know, there is this kind of cool thing happening where people do want to work the land again.

00:47:00.130 --> 00:47:01.830
 So his reaction was mixed.

00:47:02.650 --> 00:47:14.870
 But he, in the sense that we could bond over the animals, I, they're not part of my farm, but we have on the property some sheep and a pony and some chickens.

00:47:16.180 --> 00:47:18.220
 And we've had many other different animals.

00:47:20.350 --> 00:47:31.960
 His empathy for the animals, the fact that it got harder each year to slaughter his goats and pigs and sheep.

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:46.670
 His attention to detail and making sure, he was so proud of the fact, he was the last grade B dairy in the county, I want to say.

00:47:46.790 --> 00:47:52.610
 He was doing it out of an old 110-year-old redwood barn that you can still see if you drive down Wallace Road.

00:47:54.240 --> 00:48:03.800
 And he was so proud that his, I think it's the coliform count, the bacteria count in the milk, was lower than a grade A dairy.

00:48:04.520 --> 00:48:05.280
 And why?

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:10.370
 Because he did his work with a lot of intention.

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:22.180
 And he spent most of his time thinking about those animals, their welfare, and the people who would be eventually drinking that milk or eating that cheese.

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:41.360
 I remember writing Dick and Barbara postcards from Costa Rica, actually, and telling him about this amazing coffee farm that I got to live on for three months, but it felt like longer.

00:48:43.230 --> 00:48:50.710
 And so I would just, I was making a list before you came here of mentors of mine, and maybe we'll get into them.

00:48:50.790 --> 00:48:54.930
 Maybe I should just write more about them, because it's too long to get into now.

00:48:55.010 --> 00:49:05.340
 But I would be remiss to not mention Roberto Jimenez, who I lived with, and again, his wife, Noemi, they are very much a team.

00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:10.720
 Neither of them could have success without the other's hard work, just like Dick and Barbara.

00:49:11.440 --> 00:49:29.290
 Anyway, I remember writing to Dick when I was 20, and I lived down there, and writing to him and saying, you know, with this new respect, you know, Dick, going over there as a teenager, I'd help out.

00:49:29.290 --> 00:49:37.500
 I thought I was a ranch hand, whatever needed doing, I'd help out with, and I liked it, but it wasn't a very deep connection.

00:49:38.630 --> 00:49:59.890
 And I'd never lived on a true working farm until I was 20, and Roberto, I sure wish I could show him this place, because I think it's fair to say that without those three months, I don't know that it would have sparked me to farm.

00:49:59.890 --> 00:50:06.340
 I guess there's been a lot of people along the way, but, you know, here's a family similarly.

00:50:06.560 --> 00:50:16.100
 I mean, do your listeners know that coffee is the second most traded commodity on this planet behind oil?

00:50:16.480 --> 00:50:20.560
 I mean, not even behind, like, rice or wheat or corn, behind oil.

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:42.120
 Here is a small landowner farming land, five hectares, I think, in this globalized system, and dignity with which he approached it really inspired me.

00:50:42.200 --> 00:50:52.480
 And the fact that he was on his land, let's say 363 days of the year, almost every day, coffee people that he might never meet,

00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:58.630
 that they were drinking was tended by this person, these people, this family.

00:51:00.470 --> 00:51:05.870
 I know that now that I'm saying that doesn't sound that crazy, but it really opened my mind.

00:51:07.180 --> 00:51:16.140
 And I appreciate you bringing them both up in the same breath, because here I came back and saw Dick doing something very similar,

00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:22.760
 but with goats, with goat milk, which is not a global commodity, to the best of my knowledge.

00:51:24.350 --> 00:51:26.910
 And now, everywhere I look, I see it.

00:51:27.640 --> 00:51:33.600
 And I realize that that was happening before I was born, and will be happening after I'm gone.

00:51:35.400 --> 00:51:40.200
 People's love of the land is something beautiful, it's worth celebrating.

00:51:40.940 --> 00:51:49.900
 And again, it makes me feel excited about the non-fad element of renewed interest in agriculture and food.

00:51:52.610 --> 00:52:02.020
 I do feel strongly that it enriches people's lives to know a little bit about where their food came from.

00:52:02.620 --> 00:52:08.570
 And I'm going to know, speaking of those CSA newsletters, to know what else I'm interested in.

00:52:09.130 --> 00:52:15.410
 You know, they, yeah, generally speaking, I'm talking about their food, but sometimes I'm talking about a movie I saw.

00:52:16.130 --> 00:52:24.800
 Or my friends who got married, you know, here's some people I know that I love, and I was thinking about them when I picked your food.

00:52:24.800 --> 00:52:42.920
 You know, so I think that's, I think I'm done talking about Dick and Roberto right now, but let's just say really inspirational and always, often on my mind.

00:52:43.420 --> 00:52:54.260
 Awesome. And I just wanted to, one last question that I'll ask you to talk about, just your favorite crops, your most exciting crops,

00:52:54.260 --> 00:53:05.820
 because you don't just grow the same things that every farm grows. You kind of go out of your way and you have a few real gems that are dear to your heart. Can you tell me about some of them?

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:16.550
 Yeah, well, 2015 is the year of the okra for me because I didn't know you could grow okra until last year in Sonoma County.

00:53:16.670 --> 00:53:18.710
 I thought you had to be down in Louisiana or Florida.

00:53:19.270 --> 00:53:20.890
 I had no idea what it looked like.

00:53:20.890 --> 00:53:31.220
 It's such an exciting plant. It's related to coffee, excuse me, to chocolate, to cacao. It's related to cotton. It's related to hibiscus, and the flower is beautiful.

00:53:32.070 --> 00:53:37.910
 It's delicious, and people totally overestimate the sliminess. You just got to cook it right.

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:45.980
 There's a way to cook things to maximize their sliminess, but, you know, high heat, go quick.

00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:56.700
 Breading it has been great, and my nephews who have not learned, they're nine and five, they haven't learned those, you know,

00:53:56.800 --> 00:54:02.760
 they haven't heard people saying okra is gross, so they don't think it's gross. They think it's like kind of a cool green bean.

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:05.290
 So okra's been great, Devin.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:15.330
 I love my lettuce. That is a crop that has done well for me four out of five years, sells really well at the market,

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:25.840
 and I am growing an open-pollinated type that I got the seed for in 2010. It's called Devil's Ears.

00:54:27.130 --> 00:54:33.540
 I'd never grown it. I sewed it with my other lettuce, and it's really cool. It starts out kind of greenish-red,

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:39.620
 but then it starts twisting, and I'm sure that's how it got its name. It starts getting redder and redder and twisting.

00:54:40.180 --> 00:54:46.610
 I'm sure one of its relatives is a deer tongue. It's got this kind of tongue-shaped tongue or ear shape.

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:50.150
 It's almost like this Romanesco-like geometry to it.

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:56.740
 It does. It's got this spiral. You can't look at it and not get excited. Well, I can't, anyway.

00:54:57.500 --> 00:55:02.320
 But I try to impart that on my cut. I'm sure some of your listeners have bought this lettuce from me.

00:55:03.240 --> 00:55:06.890
 Actually, can I tell you something? Well, I'm just going to tell you.

00:55:07.110 --> 00:55:16.200
 When I went in for my interview for Shone at the JC, I was so filled with excitement at this lettuce.

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:23.290
 I mean, these are at least pound and a half beautiful lettuces that, you know, that morning I was out there,

00:55:23.390 --> 00:55:30.680
 and I just kind of on a whim cut it, and I brought it, and I just plopped it on the table during my final interview,

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:34.270
 which was like, you know, are you qualified for this job?

00:55:34.310 --> 00:55:40.310
 And I was kind of trying to say, this lettuce shows that I'm qualified for this job.

00:55:43.570 --> 00:55:50.200
 And so I got it. That lettuce, the point of this story is I save the seed from it every year,

00:55:50.320 --> 00:55:55.910
 and I pick the most beautiful and the tastiest and the ones that don't flower in the heat.

00:55:56.370 --> 00:56:01.490
 And I save the seed from those ones, and it pains me because I actually want to eat those or sell them.

00:56:01.830 --> 00:56:04.850
 But I keep them in the ground, and they flower, and they go to seed.

00:56:05.750 --> 00:56:08.510
 And here I am in my fifth year.

00:56:10.010 --> 00:56:16.070
 It's not a stretch or crazy to say that I'm locally adapting it, not just to Snoma County, not just to Santa Rosa,

00:56:16.210 --> 00:56:18.310
 but this little piece of land.

00:56:20.450 --> 00:56:21.740
 That's terribly exciting.

00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:25.590
 So, okay, before I keep going, so okra, the lettuce.

00:56:26.360 --> 00:56:28.480
 This year we tried a bunch of things for the first time.

00:56:28.620 --> 00:56:29.680
 Jerusalem artichokes.

00:56:29.890 --> 00:56:36.330
 We did lovage, which is not very popular, and I might not grow it again, but it's a cool flavor.

00:56:38.010 --> 00:56:46.580
 So, we did parsnips for the first time, and drumroll being we did chayotes.

00:56:47.400 --> 00:56:49.140
 And chayote, what do they call them in English?

00:56:49.340 --> 00:56:54.620
 Do they have, do people, I think one time I saw it in an old book called Pear Squash,

00:56:55.020 --> 00:56:56.540
 because they're kind of shaped like a pear.

00:56:57.520 --> 00:57:06.530
 Anyway, we, a friend of mine who grew up in Mexico 60 years ago, came up to Healdsburg when he was 17,

00:57:07.690 --> 00:57:15.940
 gave me some chayotes, and I've got a few on the plant right now, on the vine, and we love them.

00:57:16.240 --> 00:57:18.720
 My nephews love them too, which is exciting.

00:57:19.330 --> 00:57:25.980
 Joey, how can people follow along with what you're doing here, and how can people get in touch with you?

00:57:26.170 --> 00:57:29.810
 I want to answer that, and there's one more crop I want to talk about, which is the dry beans.

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:34.410
 I can't believe, I felt this, I felt this twinge of like, that I was neglecting them.

00:57:34.470 --> 00:57:41.100
 But we grow dry beans, because I don't eat meat, and I eat a lot of beans.

00:57:41.560 --> 00:57:48.920
 And these are kind of a classic, every year my CSA gets a newsletter about how important beans are.

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:50.940
 And that goes back to Roberto.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:59.470
 We had this amazing, it was like the definition of agroecology, which was like, notice the rainfall, sow these beans.

00:57:59.650 --> 00:58:01.570
 Oh, there's a great word for it, but I don't remember.

00:58:02.010 --> 00:58:04.250
 Where you sow the beans in the thatch.

00:58:04.720 --> 00:58:13.560
 You don't bury them, you poke them with a stick, but you don't really plant them in the soil.

00:58:14.120 --> 00:58:21.850
 And you time it right, and then you check on them once, and then you harvest them, and the land wasn't necessarily his.

00:58:22.490 --> 00:58:24.370
 And it was a pretty benign use of it.

00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:28.100
 I actually probably fixed some nitrogen, brought it back, dried it on the lawn.

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:29.510
 Oh, so inspiring.

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:34.800
 Dry beans, we grow them, even if it's not profitable, and we always will.

00:58:34.800 --> 00:58:44.980
 And we get people thinking about one of these most basic foods that is an amazing plant family, incredible diversity.

00:58:45.520 --> 00:58:50.860
 It's a new world crop, so I could go on and on, but I just had to shout out my beans.

00:58:51.400 --> 00:58:55.280
 Your question was, how do people find out about me?

00:58:55.440 --> 00:58:59.720
 Yeah, how do people find out about me locally on the internet, and how do people get in touch with me?

00:58:59.720 --> 00:59:03.880
 Yeah, so one of these days, we will follow the advice and get a website.

00:59:04.320 --> 00:59:05.740
 Maybe it will be this winter.

00:59:06.600 --> 00:59:11.460
 But in the meantime, we have a Facebook page, which is our website.

00:59:12.320 --> 00:59:16.080
 And that's actually kind of cool, because we put photos on there and little updates.

00:59:16.720 --> 00:59:19.540
 And that's just facebook.com slash let's go farm.

00:59:19.740 --> 00:59:20.440
 Pretty easy.

00:59:22.570 --> 00:59:26.520
 And you can find me at the Windsor Farmers Market on Sundays.

00:59:26.600 --> 00:59:38.910
 And it's a pretty good way to find me because I will continue talking your ear off about food sovereignty and dry beans and chayotes if there's not too many people behind you in line.

00:59:39.690 --> 00:59:42.750
 But I also, I mean, I could give you my phone number.

00:59:42.830 --> 00:59:51.700
 Well, I'll put a farm, farmletsgoatgmail.com is probably the better way to get a hold of me or that Facebook page.

00:59:51.920 --> 00:59:59.610
 And you can come out here and we can walk the farm and talk about what's on your mind.

00:59:59.610 --> 01:00:03.850
 And just maybe you can bring your kids out.

01:00:04.630 --> 01:00:05.810
 We really like that.

01:00:07.200 --> 01:00:12.920
 They can make some memories that involve the outdoors.

01:00:15.140 --> 01:00:20.080
 I think those are the best ways to get a hold of me, farmletsgoatgmail.com.

01:00:21.170 --> 01:00:22.470
 Joey, thank you so much.

01:00:22.790 --> 01:00:32.680
 When we, the vision for this first season of the podcast was to talk with friends who have, through their experience and through over years of knowing each other,

01:00:32.760 --> 01:00:38.920
 have really shaped the way that we think about food and food systems and visions for a more just future.

01:00:38.920 --> 01:00:43.220
 And you really, truly are one of those friends who has shaped that for me.

01:00:43.300 --> 01:00:44.180
 So thank you so much.

01:00:44.980 --> 01:00:45.820
 It's really my pleasure.

01:00:45.960 --> 01:00:47.960
 I like the name Delicious Revolution.

01:00:48.450 --> 01:00:49.720
 I really like that.

01:00:50.110 --> 01:00:51.450
 I like the model.

01:00:51.550 --> 01:00:55.430
 I can't wait to hear the other ones, the other people, you know.

01:00:56.010 --> 01:00:58.290
 And we should do it again.

01:00:58.370 --> 01:00:59.290
 We should do a part two.

01:00:59.950 --> 01:01:04.450
 And we should just keep this conversation going.

01:01:04.490 --> 01:01:06.110
 Because that's what it is.

01:01:06.490 --> 01:01:09.350
 You know, you and I have been talking, what did you say, 12 years?

01:01:09.830 --> 01:01:10.910
 It's a conversation.

01:01:10.910 --> 01:01:17.590
 And the conversation is getting more complex and exciting.

01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:21.930
 There's all these models that we didn't get to go into on this one.

01:01:22.010 --> 01:01:23.050
 But we'll do it on the next one.

01:01:23.270 --> 01:01:25.970
 And I really appreciate you asking me to be on.

01:01:26.090 --> 01:01:26.410
 Thank you.

01:01:26.530 --> 01:01:27.050
 Sounds good.

01:01:27.150 --> 01:01:28.410
 Until the next one.

01:01:28.750 --> 01:01:29.070
 All right.

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:29.810
 Sounds good.

